  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Three days have passed since the disastrous rehearsal studio party, and Andréa has heard nothing from Colette. Sitting alone at her desk, Andréa tries to lose herself in piles of romantic comedy screenplays, but every title reminds her of her heartache. When Camille stops by with the morning mail, Andréa tries to hide her mounting anxiety before spotting a familiar figure in the hallway. Listen to the scene in French first. Don't worry about catching every word.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Tu Coucheras avec Moi Ce Soir?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Merde!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Amour et Muffins.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "C'est un complot ou quoi?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Ça va?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Ouais.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "On a des nouvelles de cette Brancillon?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Ça fait trois jours qu'elle n'est pas venue.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Bizarre, non?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Elle avait besoin de papiers.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Elle n'a pas appelé?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Non.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Je vous laisse.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Madame Brancillon!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Je peux vous dire un mot?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's walk through Andréa's frustration and Colette's icy return step by step.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Tu Coucheras avec Moi Ce Soir?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Will You Sleep with Me Tonight?' The simple future 'will sleep' forms a direct proposal.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Merde!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Damn it!' Andréa tosses the script aside in sheer exasperation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Amour et Muffins.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Love and Muffins.' The conjunction 'and' links two sweet comedy tropes.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "C'est un complot ou quoi?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is it a conspiracy or what?' The colloquial tag 'or' adds sharp frustration.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Ça va?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you okay?' Camille asks the standard informal French check-in question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Ouais.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yeah.' The informal variant 'yeah' gives a clipped, sullen answer.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "On a des nouvelles de cette Brancillon?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do we have news of that Brancillon?' Notice how the verb for 'have' leads straight into 'news'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Ça fait trois jours qu'elle n'est pas venue.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It has been three days since she came.' Camille opens with the word for 'makes' and lands on 'come' at the close.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Bizarre, non?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Strange, isn't it?' Listen for the expression meaning 'strange', which leads naturally into 'no'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Elle avait besoin de papiers.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She needed papers.' Andréa opens with the word for 'had' and lands on 'papers' at the close.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Elle n'a pas appelé?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She didn't call?' The key word here means 'has', paired with 'called' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Non.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No.' Camille gives a clear and simple negative answer.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "camille",
      "t": "Je vous laisse.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I\\'ll leave you.' The polite phrase 'I' is used when taking one's leave.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Madame Brancillon!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Madame Brancillon! Wait!' Andréa calls out as the auditor marches past her office.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "lea",
      "t": "Je peux vous dire un mot?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Can I have a word with you?' Andréa opens with the word for 'can' and lands on 'word' at the close. Now listen to the whole scene again at normal speed, and see how much more you catch.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
